{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-family-selection", "file": "references/task-3ec97818-paired-fields.md", "content": "# task-3ec97818: divider-separated paired fields (unresolved instance)

Four demonstrations support an output grid formed by removing a uniform divider row/column and reducing the two adjacent fields at aligned coordinates to a sparse 0/2 mask. In demos 2 and 3, outputs appeared to mark aligned non-background cells from the two fields; demo 4 similarly marked coincident left 7s and right 3s, including suppressing left markers where the right field had 0 intrusions. However, do not infer a universal rule from this: the test had a vertical divider 9, left field background 0 with markers 7, and right field containing both 7 and 0. Two submissions encoding left-7 AND right-0 (the visually tempting foreground intersection) were both rejected. A further submission assuming overlap of 7 on both sides was also not yet confirmed in the recorded session. The correct test rule therefore remains unknown.

General diagnostic guidance for recurrences: identify divider position and remove it exactly; compare only aligned field cells; determine each field's background/foreground roles independently rather than assuming zero is background everywhere. Build an exhaustive paired-value table for every demo and compare each input pair to the output. Check semantic role combinations (foreground/foreground, foreground/background, same literal color, equality/inequality) across all demos. A rejected output falsifies the complete encoding hypothesis; never resubmit the same grid under a different rationale. Do not claim a solution absent correctness feedback.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u082/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-demo-family-selection/references/task-3ec97818-paired-fields.md"}
